{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":283365,"items":[{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5652350","title":["The Agentic AI Governance Framework: A Universal Model for Risk, Accountability, and Compliance in Autonomous Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8440-2191","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rajat","family":"Pandey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5652350","abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid and enthusiastic interest in development and deployment of Agentic AI and especially autonomous agents which are capable of independent reasoning, leveraging and&amp;nbsp; executing tools, has outpaced the definition of governance mechanism that is required to manage their operational, ethical and regulatory risks.There are existing AI governance models like NIST AI RMF, ISO\/IEC 42001, do provide foundational controls however, they are very generic and lack the implementation depth for these conitnuously acting, multi-agent systems. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;This paper introduces The Agentic AI Governance Framework, which is a six principled lifecycle model designed to ensure traceability, accountability, and regulatory alignment across agentic deployments. The framework formalizes quantifiable controls through two applied mechanisms: the Agentic Log Retention Index (ALRI), which defines evidenceretention periods based on agent risk and jurisdiction, and a Runtime Governance Architecture integrating human-in-loop escalation, tool-access allow-lists, and modelprovenance capture.&amp;nbsp;Implementation mappings are provided for major orchestration environments including LangChain, Power Platform, and UiPath.&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Adoption of the framework enables organizations to reconstruct agentic decisions within 24 hours, achieve \u2265 95 % provenance coverage, and detect behavioral drift within 10 % variance. The proposed model bridges the current gap between compliance principles and deployable governance practice, establishing a reusable baseline for safe and auditable Agentic AI operations.&lt;br&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5557141","title":["Regulatory Compliance Made Easier with AI Risk Scor The Dual Imperative in RegTech: Leveraging AI-Driven Risk Scorecards for Continuous Compliance, Operational Efficiency, and Establishing Governance Frameworks for Explainability and Ethical Accountability"],"author":[{"given":"MaryEllen","family":"O'Connell","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5557141"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_9","title":["Integrating AI Governance with Enterprise Governance Risk and Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"James","family":"Sayles","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Principles of AI Governance and Model Risk Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_9"},{"DOI":"10.37547\/tajmei\/volume08issue06-01","title":["Algorithmic Accountability in Enterprise AI Systems: A Governance Framework Integrating Risk Analytics, Cybersecurity Controls, and Ethical Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"Sadia","family":"Afroz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Information Technology services Administration and Management, St.Francis college, NY,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuvo Ranjan","family":"Das","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management and Information Technology in Healthcare Management, St.Francis College, NY, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hasib Ur","family":"Rashid","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management and Information Technology in Business Analytics, St.Francis college, NY,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"MD Al-Amin","family":"Chowdhury","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management and Information Technology in Business Analytics, St.Francis college, NY,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,4]]},"container-title":["The American Journal of Management and Economics Innovations"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.37547\/tajmei\/volume08issue06-01","abstract":"<jats:p>The exponential growth of enterprise artificial intelligence (AI) systems has brought about new levels of efficiency in decision-making, automation, and predictive analytics in fields of finance, healthcare, and supply chain management. Nevertheless, this proliferation has also increased the fear about algorithmic responsibility, especially with regard to unclear decision-making, inherent biases, cybersecurity risks, and non-compliance with regulations. Although there has been an increasing amount of research on AI governance, the current frameworks are still highly fragmented because, in most cases, they tend to focus on risk management, cybersecurity, and ethical matters separately, but not as one system. This project will create an overall governance framework operationalizing algorithmic accountability in enterprise AI settings through a systematic combination of risk analytics, cybersecurity measures, and compliance mechanisms. The study takes a conceptual and analysis approach, which summarizes the findings of the peer reviewed articles, international regulatory guidelines, and industry best practices. Comparative analysis of existing frameworks - such as those suggested by international standard-setting organizations - has shown some essential gaps in cross-domain integration, real-time monitoring, and enforceable accountability facilities. The suggested framework presents a multi-level governance structure that aligns the technical protection with the organizational supervision and auditing of ethical processes. The integration of quantitative risk assessment models, AI-specific cybersecurity controls, like adversarial robustness and secure model lifecycle management, and embedded ethical compliance mechanisms, including fairness, transparency, and explainability, are among the key contributions. The results show that a combined governance strategy is an effective way to improve the transparency, auditability, and resiliency of enterprise AI systems. The study will make a contribution to the developing discussion of AI regulation by providing a model that is scalable and implementation-focused, which can be used to help organizations make decisions and achieve regulatory alignment. The framework offers implementable lessons to businesses aiming to use AI in a responsible manner without compromising to the new global standards.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.61841\/turcomat.v15i3.15512","title":["GENERATIVE AI-ENABLED COMPLIANCE DOCUMENTATION AND AUDIT TRAIL AUTOMATION FOR GLOBAL DATA CENTER GOVERNANCE"],"author":[{"given":"Raghunath","family":"Loganathan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,31]]},"container-title":["Turkish Journal of Computer and Mathematics Education (TURCOMAT)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.61841\/turcomat.v15i3.15512","abstract":"<jats:p>Global data centers execute complex data processing activities, often across multiple jurisdictions, attracting a multitude of sector-specific compliance requirements. Faced with mounting pressure from regulators and civil society, organizations must demonstrate their ability to meet these demands. While most possess abundant technology assets, many struggle to maintain updated compliance documentation, such as privacy impact assessments and policies. Automating the generation of narrative compliance artifacts would assist teams in addressing customer requests and fulfilling reporting obligations to trusted partners. Additionally, an auditable trail of compliance-related activities, with identifiers to sources and supporting data, would help organizations respond to regulatory inquiries with less effort. Generative AI Technics are well-suited for these use cases.\r\nGenerative AI enables the creation of a wide variety of content, including text, images, and sounds. Large language models, one of the main types of Generative AI, are trained on massive datasets to understand and generate humanlike text. Following proper usage guides and user feedback, these models can generate convincingly logical responses that address the user\u2019s intent. However, they are prone to factual inaccuracies and do not understand the content they produce. Organizations seeking to leverage large language models must therefore establish guidelines and processes that control input quality, ensure consistency and correctness, and provide indications of trustworthiness and reliability.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-5","title":["Cyber-Resilient Financial Audits: Cloud-Driven Approaches to Risk and Compliance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-8739-4597","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Deepti","family":"Kushwaha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-4922-7189","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Keerti","family":"Thakur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"container-title":["The AI Audit: Accountability, Integrity, and the Future of Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-5","abstract":"<jats:p>With data becoming the key form of currency, financial audits become more exposed to cyber attacks. The move from old auditing methods to cloud solutions brings benefits as well as major issues to face. This chapter looks into how cyber-resilience supports financial audits, especially by using cloud computing to increase the accuracy, compliance, safety of data and handling of risks. A review of traditional and cloud-driven audits in this chapter supports setting up secure, compliant and tech-based ways to carry out audits. Financial services can turn to cloud platforms for efficiency and security since these platforms can help defend against cyber threats.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.14293\/pr2199.003853.v1","title":["From Retrieval to Autonomy: Governance and Accountability Challenges in Tool-Calling Large Language Model Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7063-9086","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lawal","family":"G. Anand","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,16]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14293\/pr2199.003853.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The evolution of large language models (LLMs) from passive text generators to autonomous agents capable of invoking external tools, accessing databases, executing code, and interacting with digital environments has significantly expanded their practical utility across diverse domains. While tool-calling architectures enhance problem-solving capabilities and enable dynamic decision-making, they also introduce complex governance and accountability challenges that extend beyond traditional concerns associated with standalone LLMs. Autonomous agents can perform multi-step actions with limited human intervention, creating new risks related to transparency, responsibility attribution, security vulnerabilities, privacy violations, and unintended consequences. This paper critically examines the governance implications of tool-calling LLM agents, focusing on the tension between increased autonomy and the need for effective oversight. The study explores key accountability issues, including decision traceability, auditability, explainability, human-in-the-loop control, regulatory compliance, and liability allocation among developers, deployers, and end users. Furthermore, it analyzes emerging governance frameworks and technical safeguards designed to ensure trustworthy agent behavior in high-stakes environments such as healthcare, finance, cybersecurity, and public administration. The paper argues that existing governance mechanisms developed for conventional AI systems are insufficient to address the operational complexity of autonomous tool-using agents. A multidimensional governance approach integrating technical controls, organizational policies, ethical principles, and regulatory standards is therefore proposed. The findings contribute to ongoing discussions on responsible AI deployment by highlighting pathways for balancing innovation, autonomy, and accountability in next-generation intelligent systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_1","title":["Introduction to Generative AI and Large Language Models (LLMs)"],"author":[{"given":"Banu","family":"Parasuraman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Mastering Spring AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_1"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/07366981.2026.2677215","title":["AI hallucinations as enterprise risk: Governance, audit, and accountability perspectives"],"author":[{"given":"Parul","family":"Agarwal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed to be University), Pune, Institute of Management & Research, New Delhi","place":["India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Neelam","family":"Sharma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed to be University), Pune, Institute of Management & Research, New Delhi","place":["India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minakshi","family":"Sati","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed to be University), Pune, Institute of Management & Research, New Delhi","place":["India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sanjoy","family":"Roy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Management, Bharati Vidyapeeth (Deemed to be University), Pune, Institute of Management & Research, New Delhi","place":["India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Manish","family":"Didwania","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Central University of Haryana","place":["Mahendragarh, India"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,25]]},"container-title":["EDPACS"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/07366981.2026.2677215"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1154-8_7","title":["AI Governance and Responsible AI"],"author":[{"given":"Arindam","family":"Ganguly","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Scaling Enterprise Solutions with Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1154-8_7"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7009698","title":["AI Governance Framework for Large Language Models in Healthcare: Ensuring Trust, Privacy, Security and Regulatory Compliance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-0635-4419","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mudassir Ali","family":"Mohammed","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dr Yohannes","family":"Tadesse","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7009698","abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, and Med-PaLM are transforming healthcare by enabling clinical decision support, patient engagement, and administrative automation. Yet, governance frameworks remain underdeveloped compared to traditional AI systems. This paper introduces the Enterprise LLM Governance Framework (ELGM\u00b2), a multi-layered model integrating lifecycle governance, prompt management, hallucination detection, human-in-the-loop validation, and compliance with global regulations (HIPAA, GDPR, EU AI Act, India DPDP Act, NIST AI RMF, ISO 42001). The framework embeds audit trails, explainability, and continuous monitoring to ensure trust, privacy, and accountability. By addressing the unique risks of LLMs, this research fills a critical gap and provides a roadmap for ethical and compliant adoption of generative AI in healthcare through 2030.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.32388\/a8dyj7.2","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,28]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/a8dyj7.2","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALL.E model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALL.E, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to-image (variation). Two types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment and \u201ccrop-vs-weed\u201d environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. For fruit crops, image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Conversely, in crop vs weed scenarios, image-to-image generation showed a 3.77% increase in PSNR, demonstrating enhanced image precision, but experienced a slight 0.76% decrease in FSIM, suggesting a minor reduction in feature similarity. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALL.E's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of precision agricultural solutions.\n<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.175616948.85236631\/v1","title":["Dual-Use of Large Language Models (LLMs) and Generative AI (GenAI) in Cybersecurity: Risks, Defenses, and Governance Strategies"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4058-1108","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kiarash","family":"Ahi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"LLM\/ AI Product Leader"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vaibhav","family":"Agrawal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lead Security Engineer"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saeed","family":"Valizadeh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Lead Security Researcher, Virelya Intelligence Research Labs"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,26]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.175616948.85236631\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) are profoundly reshaping the cybersecurity landscape, creating a dual-use dilemma where the same capabilities that enhance defenses also empower sophisticated offensive threats. While security teams leverage LLMs for automated threat detection, static code analysis, and DevSecOps integration, adversaries weaponize them to generate polymorphic malware, zero-day exploits, and highly scalable social engineering payloads. This survey is the first to systematically unify these offensive and defensive perspectives. Drawing on a comprehensive analysis of over 80 academic, industry, and threat intelligence sources, we project the share of LLM-assisted malware to rise from an estimated 2% of all attacks in 2021 to 50% by 2025. We synthesize key countermeasures from platforms like Google Play Protect and Microsoft Security Copilot, and we propose a robust governance roadmap that addresses the technical, ethical, and policy challenges of secure, privacypreserving, and scalable LLM deployment. Our findings position LLMs not merely as tools, but as critical infrastructure-central to both attack and defense-in the emerging AI-powered cybersecurity era. Impact Statement-This work delivers the first comprehensive framework uniting offensive and defensive perspectives on Large Language Models (LLMs) in cybersecurity. By combining technical strategies with governance requirements, it provides actionable guidance for integrating LLMs into secure development, automated threat detection, and policy enforcement. The proposed roadmap enables governments, enterprises, and security teams to deploy LLMs in ways that are scalable, privacy-preserving, and resilient against adversarial misuse. Its synthesis of cross-industry case studies, risk projections, and regulatory trends equips decision-makers with a blueprint for mitigating dual-use risks while maximizing the societal and economic benefits of AI-driven security innovation.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.67363","title":["Generative Large Language Model\u2014Powered Conversational AI App for Personalized Risk Assessment: Case Study in COVID-19 (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-2419-3101","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad Amin","family":"Roshani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0776-8045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiangyu","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2995-3385","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yao","family":"Qiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1717-7593","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Srinivasan","family":"Suresh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9579-6798","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Steven","family":"Hicks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5183-0325","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Usha","family":"Sethuraman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0225-7817","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongxiao","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,9]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.67363","abstract":"<sec>\n                    <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                        <p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in natural language tasks and are increasingly being integrated into health care for tasks like disease risk assessment. Traditional machine learning methods rely on structured data and coding, limiting their flexibility in dynamic clinical environments. This study presents a novel approach to disease risk assessment using generative LLMs through conversational artificial intelligence (AI), eliminating the need for programming.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                        <p>This study evaluates the use of pretrained generative LLMs, including LLaMA2-7b and Flan-T5-xl, for COVID-19 severity prediction with the goal of enabling a real-time, no-code, risk assessment solution through chatbot-based, question-answering interactions. To contextualize their performance, we compare LLMs with traditional machine learning classifiers, such as logistic regression, extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and random forest, which rely on tabular data.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                        <p>We fine-tuned LLMs using few-shot natural language examples from a dataset of 393 pediatric patients, developing a mobile app that integrates these models to provide real-time, no-code, COVID-19 severity risk assessment through clinician-patient interaction. The LLMs were compared with traditional classifiers across different experimental settings, using the area under the curve (AUC) as the primary evaluation metric. Feature importance derived from LLM attention layers was also analyzed to enhance interpretability.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                        <p>Generative LLMs demonstrated strong performance in low-data settings. In zero-shot scenarios, the T0-3b-T model achieved an AUC of 0.75, while other LLMs, such as T0pp(8bit)-T and Flan-T5-xl-T, reached 0.67 and 0.69, respectively. At 2-shot settings, logistic regression and random forest achieved an AUC of 0.57, while Flan-T5-xl-T and T0-3b-T obtained 0.69 and 0.65, respectively. By 32-shot settings, Flan-T5-xl-T reached 0.70, similar to logistic regression (0.69) and random forest (0.68), while XGBoost improved to 0.65. These results illustrate the differences in how generative LLMs and traditional models handle the increasing data availability. LLMs perform well in low-data scenarios, whereas traditional models rely more on structured tabular data and labeled training examples. Furthermore, the mobile app provides real-time, COVID-19 severity assessments and personalized insights through attention-based feature importance, adding value to the clinical interpretation of the results.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                                <sec>\n                    <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                        <p>Generative LLMs provide a robust alternative to traditional classifiers, particularly in scenarios with limited labeled data. Their ability to handle unstructured inputs and deliver personalized, real-time assessments without coding makes them highly adaptable to clinical settings. This study underscores the potential of LLM-powered conversational artificial intelligence (AI) in health care and encourages further exploration of its use for real-time, disease risk assessment and decision-making support.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-1-4842-9367-6_5","title":["Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Tom","family":"Taulli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-1-4842-9367-6_5"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/67363","title":["Generative Large Language Model\u2014Powered Conversational AI App for Personalized Risk Assessment: Case Study in COVID-19"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-2419-3101","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad Amin","family":"Roshani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0776-8045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiangyu","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2995-3385","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yao","family":"Qiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1717-7593","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Srinivasan","family":"Suresh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9579-6798","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Steven","family":"Hicks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5183-0325","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Usha","family":"Sethuraman","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0225-7817","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dongxiao","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,27]]},"container-title":["JMIR AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/67363","abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Background<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated powerful capabilities in natural language tasks and are increasingly being integrated into health care for tasks like disease risk assessment. Traditional machine learning methods rely on structured data and coding, limiting their flexibility in dynamic clinical environments. This study presents a novel approach to disease risk assessment using generative LLMs through conversational artificial intelligence (AI), eliminating the need for programming.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Objective<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>This study evaluates the use of pretrained generative LLMs, including LLaMA2-7b and Flan-T5-xl, for COVID-19 severity prediction with the goal of enabling a real-time, no-code, risk assessment solution through chatbot-based, question-answering interactions. To contextualize their performance, we compare LLMs with traditional machine learning classifiers, such as logistic regression, extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and random forest, which rely on tabular data.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>We fine-tuned LLMs using few-shot natural language examples from a dataset of 393 pediatric patients, developing a mobile app that integrates these models to provide real-time, no-code, COVID-19 severity risk assessment through clinician-patient interaction. The LLMs were compared with traditional classifiers across different experimental settings, using the area under the curve (AUC) as the primary evaluation metric. Feature importance derived from LLM attention layers was also analyzed to enhance interpretability.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Results<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Generative LLMs demonstrated strong performance in low-data settings. In zero-shot scenarios, the T0-3b-T model achieved an AUC of 0.75, while other LLMs, such as T0pp(8bit)-T and Flan-T5-xl-T, reached 0.67 and 0.69, respectively. At 2-shot settings, logistic regression and random forest achieved an AUC of 0.57, while Flan-T5-xl-T and T0-3b-T obtained 0.69 and 0.65, respectively. By 32-shot settings, Flan-T5-xl-T reached 0.70, similar to logistic regression (0.69) and random forest (0.68), while XGBoost improved to 0.65. These results illustrate the differences in how generative LLMs and traditional models handle the increasing data availability. LLMs perform well in low-data scenarios, whereas traditional models rely more on structured tabular data and labeled training examples. Furthermore, the mobile app provides real-time, COVID-19 severity assessments and personalized insights through attention-based feature importance, adding value to the clinical interpretation of the results.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>\n                  <jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Conclusions<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>Generative LLMs provide a robust alternative to traditional classifiers, particularly in scenarios with limited labeled data. Their ability to handle unstructured inputs and deliver personalized, real-time assessments without coding makes them highly adaptable to clinical settings. This study underscores the potential of LLM-powered conversational artificial intelligence (AI) in health care and encourages further exploration of its use for real-time, disease risk assessment and decision-making support.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>"},{"DOI":"10.15837\/aijjs.v20i1.7660","title":["RISK-AWARE LEGAL GOVERNANCE FOR AI-DRIVEN CYBERSECURITY SYSTEMS: FRAMEWORKS, COMPLIANCE, AND ACCOUNTABILITY"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-7780-3097","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Turkan","family":"Bashirli","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]},"container-title":["AGORA INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF JURIDICAL SCIENCES"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.15837\/aijjs.v20i1.7660","abstract":"<jats:p>As AI becomes increasingly central to cybersecurity, organizations face new legal and ethical challenges. This paper explores risk-aware governance approaches for AI-driven cybersecurity systems, focusing on compliance, accountability, and regulatory alignment. By reviewing current laws and AI-specific regulations, it identifies potential gaps and liabilities that may arise from automated decision-making. A practical framework is proposed to help organizations integrate risk management into their AI cybersecurity strategies, including ongoing monitoring, transparent reporting, and ethical safeguards. The study shows that considering legal and ethical aspects from the start not only reduces regulatory risks but also strengthens trust and system reliability. The insights offered aim to guide policymakers, legal professionals, and technology developers in fostering responsible AI adoption in cybersecurity.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202604.0956.v1","title":["AI Disclosure Without Accountability: Paper Compliance and the Governance Limits of Transparency in Scientific Research"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-9560-3927","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Victor","family":"Frimpong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,14]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202604.0956.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>This paper argues that the institutionalisation of artificial intelligence (AI) disclosure in scientific research has resulted in a type of compliance that emphasises symbolic transparency rather than actual responsibility. Although journals and publishers are progressively mandating that authors disclose their use of AI, these policies remain fragmented, non-standardised, and largely unverifiable. Based on an exploratory review of 80 recent academic articles, the report demonstrates that explicit AI disclosure is limited and, when present, is primarily symbolic or narrative rather than verifiable. This work explains the pattern by introducing the concept of paper compliance and establishing the AI Disclosure Integrity Gap (AIDG), which is defined as the discrepancy between reported AI utilisation and the genuine epistemic impact of AI on research results. The analysis reveals that this disparity is systematically generated by the discordance between transparency-oriented governance frameworks and the iterative, opaque, and irreproducible characteristics of AI-assisted knowledge generation. The study develops testable propositions and introduces the AI Use Traceability Framework (AUTF) as a process-oriented approach for AI governance, emphasising traceability and auditability over transparency. Despite institutional, technical, and incentive-based obstacles that hinder full implementation, traceability provides a means to reduce AIDG and enhance accountability in AI-assisted research. The study advances AI governance and research integrity by treating disclosure as a limited mechanism rather than a complete solution, and by highlighting the risk that current practices create a false sense of transparency.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_8","title":["Managing and Addressing AI Compliance"],"author":[{"given":"James","family":"Sayles","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Principles of AI Governance and Model Risk Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0983-5_8"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6129286","title":["Explainable AI in U.S. Banking Fraud Detection: A Comparative Framework for Regulatory Compliance, Model Transparency, and Risk Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Mehedi","family":"Mostafa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6129286","abstract":"<jats:p>The increasing adoption of machine learning models in U.S. banking fraud detection has raised critical concerns regarding model transparency, regulatory compliance, and risk governance. While explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) techniques have been proposed to address these challenges, financial institutions continue to face uncertainty regarding the selection, evaluation, and governance of appropriate explanation methods across diverse fraud-detection use cases. This study presents a comparative and governance-oriented framework for explainable AI in U.S. banking systems, synthesizing model-agnostic and model-specific explanation techniques commonly used in fraud analytics. Building upon prior application-focused research on interpretable transaction modeling and agebased fraud analysis, this paper shifts the focus from individual implementations to a system-level evaluation of explainability methods under regulatory and operational constraints. The proposed framework evaluates explainable AI approaches across dimensions of transparency, stability, regulatory interpretability, bias awareness, and operational risk management. The study further discusses alignment with U.S. financial regulatory expectations and outlines practical guidance for responsible model deployment in fraud-detection environments. By providing a comparative and governance-centered perspective, this paper contributes a practical reference for financial institutions, policymakers, and analytics practitioners seeking to implement explainable AI solutions that balance predictive performance with accountability and regulatory compliance in U.S. banking fraud detection.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.32388\/a8dyj7","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,25]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32388\/a8dyj7","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALL.E model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALL.E, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to-image (variation). Six types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. The image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALL.E's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of imaging-based precision agricultural solutions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.30574\/wjarr.2025.25.3.0781","title":["AI-driven data governance in banking: Leveraging large language models for compliance and risk management"],"author":[{"family":"Rajesh Kamisetty","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Raj Nagamangalam","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,30]]},"container-title":["World Journal of Advanced Research and Reviews"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.30574\/wjarr.2025.25.3.0781","abstract":"<jats:p>The banking sector has to deal with governance, compliance, and risk management challenges due to the evolving nature of the financial regulation and high volume of\u2002sensitive data. Real-time monitoring and anomaly detection are challenging in traditional rule based\u2002systems, which lead to inefficiencies and compliance risks. Using Large Language Models (LLMs), this paper discusses enabling banking data governance by automating compliance with banking regulations, risk assessment\u2002and fraud detection. Allow Intelligent data classification, predictive analytics and real-time auditing, in compliance with GDPR,\u2002Basel III, AML directive standards, etc. LLMs offer a transformative solution for secure and transparent financial operations, albeit with challenges like data privacy, model\u2002bias, explainability, etc. This research is based on real case studies\u2002and discusses how AI-based data governance can provide banks with improved security, compliance with regulatory mandates, and operational effectiveness<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6840458","title":["How AI accountability governance drives innovation in Cross-Border B2B E-Commerce: A dynamic capabilities perspective on proactive risk management and digital compliance management"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7163-9442","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ting","family":"Lin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3452-3768","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chang-Bong","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2490-9246","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Xintong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6840458","abstract":"<jats:p>As AI\u00a0becomes embedded in platform-based cross-border B2B exchange, algorithmic opacity and blurred accountability increasingly threaten inter-organizational trust and constrain value creation. However, it remains unclear how accountability-oriented governance can be leveraged to convert AI-related uncertainty into innovation outcomes in CBEC\u00a0networks. Drawing on dynamic capabilities theory, this study conceptualizes\u00a0 AIAG\u00a0as a governance-oriented dynamic capability and examines its effects on innovation performance through PRM\u00a0and DCM.The results show that AIAG significantly enhances PRM, which in turn improves innovation performance both directly and indirectly through DCM. However,\u00a0 governance-driven risk proactiveness does not automatically translate into compliance embedding.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6845533","title":["How AI accountability governance drives innovation in Cross-Border B2B E-Commerce: A dynamic capabilities perspective on proactive risk management and digital compliance management"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-7163-9442","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ting","family":"Lin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-3452-3768","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chang-Bong","family":"Kim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-2490-9246","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Xintong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6845533","abstract":"<jats:p>As AI\u00a0becomes embedded in platform-based cross-border B2B exchange, algorithmic opacity and blurred accountability increasingly threaten inter-organizational trust and constrain value creation. However, it remains unclear how accountability-oriented governance can be leveraged to convert AI-related uncertainty into innovation outcomes in CBEC\u00a0networks. Drawing on dynamic capabilities theory, this study conceptualizes\u00a0 AIAG\u00a0as a governance-oriented dynamic capability and examines its effects on innovation performance through PRM\u00a0and DCM.The results show that AIAG significantly enhances PRM, which in turn improves innovation performance both directly and indirectly through DCM. However,\u00a0 governance-driven risk proactiveness does not automatically translate into compliance embedding.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6879502","title":["Experience Over Compliance: Why Frequent Use -Not Governance -Builds Managerial Trust in Generative AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7764-7106","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Cheryl","family":"Cheng Xin Yi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6879502","abstract":"<jats:p>As a digital transformation leader working within one of Asia's most regulated financial environments, the author observed first-hand how organisations invested heavily in governance infrastructure as the primary response to GenAI adoption-and questioned whether that investment was producing the managerial confidence it was designed to build. That question became the foundation of this doctoral research, which sought to bring empirical rigour to a gap the author encountered not in the literature, but in practice. This working paper is derived from doctoral dissertation research completed at Golden Gate University. The full dissertation is available via ProQuest (forthcoming, June 2026). The author wishes to thank her dissertation supervisors, Dr. Ira Chatterjee and Dr. Bruce Wasserman, for their scholarly guidance throughout the research process, and the 100 managers who gave their time and candour as study participants.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4872967","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments Using DALL.E: Generative AI-Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4872967"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/cfl.2024.4","title":["Navigating China\u2019s regulatory approach to generative artificial intelligence and large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7076-635X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mimi","family":"Zou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lu","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Cambridge Forum on AI: Law and Governance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/cfl.2024.4","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>The rapid development of generative artificial intelligence (AI) systems, particularly those fuelled by increasingly advanced large language models, has raised concerns of their potential risks among policymakers globally. In July 2023, Chinese regulators enacted the Interim Measures for the Management of Generative AI Services (\u201cthe Measures\u201d). The Measures aim to mitigate various risks associated with public-facing generative AI services, particularly those concerning information content safety and security. China\u2019s approach to regulating AI to date has sought to address the risks associated with rapidly advancing AI technologies while fostering innovation and development. Tensions between these policy objectives are reflected in the provisions of the Measures. As Beijing moves towards establishing a comprehensive legal framework for AI governance, there will be growing interest in how China\u2019s approach may influence AI governance and regulation at a global level.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4770726","title":["Creating Image Datasets in Agricultural Environments using DALL.E: Generative AI- Powered Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5417-6744","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ranjan","family":"Sapkota","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4201-0621","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Dawood","family":"Ahmed","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5337-4848","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Manoj","family":"Karkee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4770726","abstract":"<jats:p>This research investigated the role of artificial intelligence (AI), specifically the DALLE model by OpenAI, in advancing data generation and visualization techniques in agriculture. DALLE, an advanced AI image generator, works alongside ChatGPT's language processing to transform text descriptions and image clues into realistic visual representations of the content. The study used both approaches of image generation: text-to-image and image-to image (variation). Six types of datasets depicting fruit crop environment were generated. These AI-generated images were then compared against ground truth images captured by sensors in real agricultural fields. The comparison was based on Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR) and Feature Similarity Index (FSIM) metrics. The image-to-image generation exhibited a 5.78% increase in average PSNR over text-to-image methods, signifying superior image clarity and quality. However, this method also resulted in a 10.23% decrease in average FSIM, indicating a diminished structural and textural similarity to the original images. Similar to these measures, human evaluation also showed that images generated using image-to-image-based method were more realistic compared to those generated with text-to-image approach. The results highlighted DALLE's potential in generating realistic agricultural image datasets and thus accelerating the development and adoption of imaging-based precision agricultural solutions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-2827-0_18","title":["Governance and Compliance in LLM Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Ankush","family":"Sharma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Observability for Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-2827-0_18"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5126542","title":["Legal Compliance in Corporate Governance Frameworks: Best Practices for Ensuring Transparency, Accountability, and Risk Mitigation"],"author":[{"given":"Kayode","family":"Akinsola","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5126542"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6596080","title":["The AI Closed System Fallacy: How Legal Discourse Misidentifies Risk In Generative AI Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Susan","family":"Tanner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6596080","abstract":"<jats:p>This Article identifies and critiques what it terms the AI Closed System Fallacy: the widespread assumption that generative AI systems are primarily shaped by user inputs and that legal risk therefore flows mainly from prompting behavior. This assumption underlies ethics opinions, institutional policies, and regulatory guidance emphasizing confidentiality, client data input, and downstream outputs.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6059757","title":["Cybersecurity Risk Assessment And Audit Frameworks:&amp;nbsp;A Study Of Compliance And Governance"],"author":[{"given":"Omotomiwa","family":"Omolere","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6059757"},{"DOI":"10.70715\/jitcai.2025.v2.i2.022","title":["MACHINE LEARNING (ML) TO EVALUATE GOVERNANCE, RISK, AND COMPLIANCE (GRC) RISKS ASSOCIATED WITH LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS (LLMs)"],"author":[{"given":"Upakar","family":"Bhatta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/049c8eh51","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Central Washington University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,29]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Technology, Cybersecurity, and Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.70715\/jitcai.2025.v2.i2.022","abstract":"<jats:p>In today\u2019s AI-driven digital world, Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) has become vital for organizations as they leverage AI technologies to drive business success and resilience. GRC represents a strategic approach that helps organization using Large Language Models (LLMs) automation tasks and enhances customer service, while maintaining the regulatory complexity across various industries and regions. This paper explores a machine learning approach to evaluate Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) risks associated with Large Language Models (LLMs). It utilizes Azure OpenAI Service logs to construct a representative dataset, with key features including\u00a0 response_time_ms, model_type, temperature, tokens_used, is_logged, data_sensitivity, compliance_flag, bias_score, and toxicity_score. These features are used\u00a0to train a model that predicts GRC risk levels in LLM interactions, enabling organizations to improve efficiency, foster innovation, and deliver customer value, while maintaining compliance and regulatory requirements.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1142\/9781800616578_0001","title":["Ethical Governance of Generative AI: Accountability, Oversight, and Societal Impact"],"author":[{"given":"Tereza Raquel","family":"Merlo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of North Texas, Denton, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Kay","family":"Alwert","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Alwert GmbH & Co. KG, Berlin, Germany"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["AI-Driven Revolution"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1142\/9781800616578_0001"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6624698","title":["An Enterprise AI Governance Maturity Model for Cloud-deployed Generative AI Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-5523-4401","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rahul","family":"Modi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6624698","abstract":"<jats:p>Scalable cloud services, built-in security tooling, and accelerating competition are the factors behind the rapid enterprise integration of Generative AI into Microsoft Azure ecosystems (Ettinger; Biswas). Organizations are starting to use Azure-native GenAI to make their operations more productive, improve analytics, and engage with customers, and address emerging governance and risk management challenges (Biswas; Schiros et al.). It is a case of the creation of a systematic AI governance maturity model with a specific focus on Azure-based Generative AI implementations (Wairagade and Govindarajan; Rangan and Yoost). It was intended to have the enterprise goals to match the patterns of the Azure architecture, platform services, and realities of operations in various organizational settings (Rangan and Yoost; Ettinger). The methodology is a literature review and synthesis of enterprise architecture literature, developed governance frameworks, and operational practice of cloud GenAI (Denni-Fiberesima; Marchena Sekli and De La Vega). Integration of strategic, technical and organizational perspectives was done through comparative analysis to create a coherent and action-oriented maturity structure (Denni-Fiberesima; Wairagade and Govindarajan). The most important observations show that there are six dimensions of core governance capabilities, namely: strategy, architecture, security, compliance, operations and value realization (MODEL, 2024; Kongsten and Kathirgamadas). These dimensions indicate common enterprise pain points in large scale Azure GenAI deployments and regulatory landscapes (Kongsten and Kathirgamadas; Biswas). The maturity model categorizes capabilities into stages of progression supporting the controlled experimentation, standardized deployment, and optimization on an enterprise-wide level (MODEL, 2024; Rangan and Yoost). The main finding is that a progressive model of governance maturity makes it possible to adopt Generative AI in an Azure environment in a scaled, secure, and responsible way (Biswas; Schiros et al.).<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5551194","title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence (AI) and the Future of Accounting Competencies: Quantitative Evidence from the Large Language Model (LLM) Revolution"],"author":[{"given":"Dulani","family":"Jayasuriya","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5551194"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v2","title":["Imperfectly Perfect AI Chatbots: Limitations of Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v2","abstract":"<jats:p>Generative AI has transformed the landscape of AI chatbots in a way that they are increasingly becoming human-like in their understanding and working. Generative AI is used for creating new contents, and most modern AI chatbots are designed using two types of generative AI models: Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs). LLMs deal with the text, whereas LMMs deal with more modalities including text, image, audio and video. This continuous enhancement of generative AI models is bringing AI chatbots closer to the human working. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots are not perfect, as they have several limitations, and their users should comprehend before fully relying on these imperfectly perfect AI chatbots. Depending on the type of use of AI chatbots, these limitations may or may not impact them significantly. This paper will organise the limitations of AI chatbots into six main categories: intelligence and understanding related limitations; accuracy and credibility related limitations; ethics and regulations related limitations; accountability, transparency and consistency related limitations; design, coding and training related limitations; and human, machine and vender related limitations. The aim of this paper is to emphasise, organise and analyse numerous limitations of AI chatbots into the proposed categories; accordingly, users should become more cognisant about all these limitations in deciding their suitability or unsuitability for the specific use cases.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v1","title":["Imperfectly Perfect AI Chatbots: Limitations of Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121396.62005552\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>Generative AI has transformed the landscape of AI chatbots in a way that they are increasingly becoming human-like in their understanding and working. Generative AI is used for creating new contents, and most modern AI chatbots are designed using two types of generative AI models: Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs). LLMs deal with the text, whereas LMMs deal with more modalities including text, image, audio and video. This continuous enhancement of generative AI models is bringing AI chatbots closer to the human working. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots are not perfect, as they have several limitations, and their users should comprehend before fully relying on these imperfectly perfect AI chatbots. Depending on the type of use of AI chatbots, these limitations may or may not impact them significantly. This paper will organise the limitations of AI chatbots into six main categories: intelligence and understanding related limitations; accuracy and credibility related limitations; ethics and regulations related limitations; accountability, transparency and consistency related limitations; design, coding and training related limitations; and human, machine and vender related limitations. The aim of this paper is to emphasise, organise and analyse numerous limitations of AI chatbots into the proposed categories; accordingly, users should become more cognisant about all these limitations in deciding their suitability or unsuitability for the specific use cases.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6764658","title":["Compliance Ontologies for Automated AI Audit and Governance in Healthcare Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-6201-7620","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rama Krishna Kumar","family":"Lingamgunta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Krishna","family":"Chaitanya Bandaru","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Himaja","family":"Vankayala","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6764658","abstract":"<jats:p>Clinical decision support, diagnostics, patient monitoring, and administrative automation are represented and being implemented in healthcare through the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems. Nonetheless, the use of AI in the regulated healthcare settings brings forth the issue of inherent concerns about transparency, accountability, compliance and governance. The healthcare organizations are required to observe strict regulatory measures, data security policies and ethical considerations and the implementation of AI technologies safely. The manual auditing process used in the traditional models of governance is tedious and prone to errors and is inadequate to the complex systems that use AI. In this research, an ontology-based compliance model will be suggested to assist in an automated AI audit and governance in healthcare systems. It is proposed that the suggested model will facilitate the expression of regulatory policies, ethical principles and operating rules formally using compliance ontologies to conduct automated reasoning to monitor the behavior of an AI system in real time. The framework combines policy-constricted AI models, explainable AI\/mechanisms and enterprise governance frameworks\/structures to provide transparency and traceability. The study also takes a conceptual modeling and architecture design methodology to create the compliance ontology, and how it can assist in cross industry regulatory alignment. The results show that compliance management, based instead on ontology, could greatly increase compliance with the regulations, impress auditability and increase the confidence in the healthcare AI systems. The research advances the current research community on trustworthy AI by offering a systemic governance system of regulated healthcare dispensation that is scaled and automated.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781420072488-21","title":["Identity Management Audit and Attestation"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2008,4,9]]},"container-title":["Oracle Identity Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781420072488-21"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781118269145.ch13","title":["Role of Internal Audit in Enterprise Risk Management"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2011,8,22]]},"container-title":["COSO Enterprise Risk Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781118269145.ch13"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173121409.91676949\/v1","title":["Applications of AI Chatbots Based on Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,10]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173121409.91676949\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>AI chatbots are progressively becoming more human-like in their creativity and intelligence. Predominantly, AI chatbots based on generative AI models such as Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Model (LMMs) are a significant breakthrough in AI, as they have become more accurate, creative, context-aware, and can better mimic human-like interactions. Generative AI is an umbrella term for all AI technologies used to generate contents, where LLMs can process and generate textual data, and LMMs can process and generate multiple types of data modalities which are also known as multimodal AI models. AI chatbots and multimodal AI models offer several benefits, such as speed, accuracy, creativity, efficiency, and versatility; therefore, they can be used in nearly every sector such as a content creator, proactive assistant, reliable companion, customer assistant, and business operations facilitator. Nonetheless, these AI chatbots and multimodal AI models have some limitations, specially, inaccuracy and hallucination; that can affect and limit their usage for any critical task in any application area. Therefore, human verification and supervision is required for its reliable, safe and guided use. Moreover, their use in any application area also requires appropriate ethical consideration and regulatory compliance, alongside the human verification of generated responses. This paper will present twenty-two applications of AI chatbots and multimodal AI models, which are organised into two categories: personal applications and organisational applications. Subsequently, it will also describe the crucial ethical consideration and regulatory compliance for each application area to emphasise its related ethics, regulations and other risks for humans.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/books978-3-7258-7704-1","title":["Generative AI and Large Language Models"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/books978-3-7258-7704-1"},{"DOI":"10.14293\/pr2199.002326.v1","title":["Strategic Adoption of Generative AI in U.S. Manufacturing: Balancing Innovation Acceleration with Trade, Compliance, and Intellectual Property Governance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-7063-9086","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lawal","family":"G. Anand","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.14293\/pr2199.002326.v1","abstract":"<jats:p>\n                  <jats:bold>\n                    <jats:bold>The integration of generative AI into U.S. manufacturing environments introduces both innovation potential and regulatory complexity. This paper proposes a compliance-aware architecture for deploying generative AI models within industrial design and production workflows. The framework defines technical control layers, including model access governance, embedded compliance screening, and data-classification mechanisms to prevent model leakage, safeguard intellectual property, and ensure adherence to U.S. export control regulations. Unlike prior studies focused solely on AI performance or policy analysis, this work outlines a system-level implementation model combining generative design automation, secure model orchestration, and risk-aware data pipelines. Through scenario-based modeling and workflow analysis, the study demonstrates how manufacturers can accelerate innovation while maintaining governance integrity across digital supply chains. The proposed architecture bridges AI engineering, cybersecurity, and regulatory design, enabling responsible adoption of generative AI in complex manufacturing ecosystems.<\/jats:bold>\n                  <\/jats:bold>\n                <\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2118\/221872-ms","title":["Generative AI and Large Language Model Assisted Causal Discovery and Inference for Driving Process Improvements"],"author":[{"given":"P.","family":"Rai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Jain","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Anand","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Baker Hughes, Houston, Texas, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,4]]},"container-title":["ADIPEC"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2118\/221872-ms","abstract":"<jats:p>Data-driven process management coupled with machine learning have been successful in driving commercial value to oil and gas operators by offering insights into process disruptions and their root causes. One frequently used approach is to analyze causes of process disruptions exclusively from historical data. In general, specific insights in the form of high correlation between certain process performance indicators and a well-defined measure of production inefficiency is often confounded as responsible causal factors. While this may yield some insights, the complexity of processes, measured in terms of number of entities involved and their interrelationships, requires a more nuanced approach that must include the context of the specific process. Thus, data analysis must be augmented with significant inputs from experts. Causal Inference provides a conceptual framework and tools for doing such analysis.<\/jats:p>\n               <jats:p>In causal analysis, we embed this specific knowledge of subject matter experts using causal graphs consisting of process features (nodes) and their dependency (directed edges). For complex processes however, constructing causal graphs could be non-trivial due to ambiguity over which nodes to include and the plausible direction of their relationships. With the advent of foundational Large Language Models (LLM), there is an opportunity to mitigate this problem by utilizing the enormous information it encodes. Tools and technologies now exist to customize the response of LLM using retrieval of information from a corpus of specific high-quality knowledge in the form of related literature and data. It can therefore be used to assist the domain expert in building and finetuning the causal graph, and in simpler cases, can completely automate this step.<\/jats:p>\n               <jats:p>In this work, we propose a two-step approach to combine the power of LLMs and Causal Analysis for analyzing inefficiencies in production processes. In the first step, we implement a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) enhanced LLM prompting on a curated dataset designed to answer specific questions on relationship between process performance indicators. The outcome of this step is a directed acyclic graph encoding dependency of process performance indicators. Domain experts can validate or potentially refine the LLM-generated causal graph based on their domain knowledge for eliminating spurious hallucinations. In the second step, we use an appropriate causal inference method on the refined causal diagram and historical production data to estimate the causal effect of process variable contributing to disruptions or inefficiencies. Thus, by combining human expertise with machine learning, this framework offers a comprehensive approach for optimizing production processes.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.52783\/jisem.v10i50s.14229","title":["Explainable AI (XAI) Governance in CCaaS Platforms: A Trust, Compliance, and Accountability Model for Enterprise Contact Centers"],"author":[{"family":"Vipin Kalra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,27]]},"container-title":["Journal of Information Systems Engineering and Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.52783\/jisem.v10i50s.14229","abstract":"<jats:p>The proliferation of artificial intelligence (AI) in Contact Center as a Service (CCaaS) platforms has introduced significant operational efficiencies but raised critical concerns regarding transparency, compliance, and stakeholder trust. This paper presents a comprehensive Explainable AI (XAI) Gover- nance Framework specifically designed for enterprise contact centers, addressing the imperatives of trust, regulatory compli- ance, and algorithmic accountability. We propose a three-pillar model encompassing technical explainability mechanisms, regu- latory compliance protocols, and stakeholder trust frameworks. The methodology integrates SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlana- tions) and LIME (Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explana- tions) for model interpretability, continuous bias monitoring sys- tems, and real-time audit trail generation. Empirical validation across three multinational organizations demonstrates substantial improvements: 67% enhancement in compliance audit readiness, 81% reduction in regulatory findings, and 46-90% improvement in stakeholder trust metrics. The framework addresses GDPR, CCPA, and EU AI Act requirements while maintaining system performance with explanation latencies under 2 seconds. This research contributes a practical, scalable governance model for responsible AI deployment in customer-facing enterprise systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7127098","title":["Cognitive Drift in Generative AI: Self-Normalized Displacement as a Measurement Principle for Semantic Coherence in Large Language Model Outputs"],"author":[{"given":"Tatu","family":"Lertola","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7127098","abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;Hallucination in large language models (LLMs) has been predominantly treated as a surface-level error: factually incorrect content to be filtered post-generation. We propose a reframing: hallucination as loss of internal orientation, a systemic phenomenon rooted in drift, stagnation, and overconfident generation under narrowing awareness. We introduce the Volatility Factor (VF), a trajectory-level coherence signal, and the Stagnation Signal (Sg), which detects generation that continues without directional progress. Both are computed from embedding geometry alone, requiring no access to model internals.&lt;\/p&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Beyond the framework, we identify and provide initial empirical support for a candidate measurement principle: self-normalized displacement. Behavioral integrity, we propose, cannot be assessed on an absolute scale; it is measurable only as displacement from the system's own established baseline. The same normalization that resolves per-request prompt-injection detection in our prior Intent Vectoring work (the deviation ratio) also resolves trajectory-level drift detection (the displacement ratio): in a controlled multi-turn evaluation (4 conversation tracks x 2 open-weight models, 100 turns), absolute signal levels failed to separate drifting from aligned conversations, while the displacement ratio classified all eight series correctly, with an online variant detecting both drift series at zero false positives. We report the formula's full evolution including two instructive failures, recalibrate Sg for multi-turn settings, and present a calibrated phase detection grid. The framework positions per-request, per-session, and prospectively per-lifetime monitoring as instances of one measurement principle at three timescales. We present the principle as a working hypothesis, the instrument as functional but unrefined, and devote explicit sections to the maturity stage of the research and to what the instrument structurally cannot see.&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s44290-026-00474-2","title":["A governance audit framework for large language model advice in civil infrastructure decision-making illustrated with local risk models in Sugar Land, Texas"],"author":[{"given":"Alence","family":"Poudel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Trevor","family":"Surface","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,29]]},"container-title":["Discover Civil Engineering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44290-026-00474-2"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9783111425078-001","title":["1 Unveiling the Power of Generative AI: A Journey into Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"A.","family":"Ashwini","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"J.","family":"Jency Rubia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"H.","family":"Sehina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"B.","family":"Sundaravadivazhagan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,9]]},"container-title":["Generative AI and LLMs"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9783111425078-001"},{"DOI":"10.31228\/osf.io\/bheqw","title":["Generative AI Large Language Models and Researching the Law"],"author":[{"given":"Paul Douglas","family":"Callister","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,16]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31228\/osf.io\/bheqw","abstract":"<p>Generative AI Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly influencing the legal research landscape, challenging the traditional cognitive authority within the profession. This article explores how these models, despite their potential to simplify the vast amount of legal information, may also introduce risks of misinformation due to AI \"hallucinations\" and the advantages and limitations of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) systems. By examining various legal research problems, the article highlights both the capabilities and shortcomings of generative AI in legal contexts. It emphasizes the need for vigilance, critical thinking, and a continued reliance on traditional research methods to mitigate the risks associated with over-reliance on AI-generated content. As the legal profession confronts an overwhelming influx of information, the role of AI in legal research will likely grow, making it crucial to balance cognitive ease with accuracy and reliability.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4761694","title":["Exploring Mathematical Spaces using Generative AI and Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1578-2175","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mohammad","family":"Raeini","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4761694","abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models (LLMs) and generative AI (GenAI) technologies have recently reached to unprecedented capabilities thanks to rich mathematical spaces and rigorous mathematical transformations and techniques. Such models can now be used for automating certain human-level tasks such as text summarization, knowledge extraction from huge digital data, and even more. In particular, LLMs and GenAI technologies have the potential to be used for achieving one of the foundational goals of artificial intelligence, namely to develop AI systems that can do mathematical\/logical reasoning.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;In this article, we promote the idea of using generative AI and LLMs for advancing mathematical research. We discuss that language models and AI technologies can be used for exploring mathematical spaces. AI and LLMs have the potential to push forward the boundaries of human knowledge, including human\u2019s understanding of mathematical spaces, structures, objects, and transformations. In the same vein that computation emerged out (or spun off) of mathematics and accelerated mathematical research, large language models rely heavily on mathematical spaces and transformations on such spaces. Just like computation, LLMs can can help us boost our knowledge of mathematical spaces and to potentially open up new horizons on the abstract world of mathematical objects, structures, and spaces.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch13","title":["Ensuring Compliance and Ethical Standards with Generative AI in Fintech"],"author":[{"given":"Vishal","family":"Jain","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Archan","family":"Mitra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch13"},{"DOI":"10.55092\/let20240008","title":["Mitigating bias in generative AI: a comprehensive framework for governance and accountability"],"author":[{"given":"Shuchen","family":"Tang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haoming","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Law, Ethics &amp; Technology"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.55092\/let20240008"},{"DOI":"10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-8","title":["Strategic HR Technology Transformation: Bridging Compliance and Financial Audit Readiness"],"author":[{"name":"Manager, Digital Product, CVS Health, Charlotte NC, USA","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9785-9497","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Manoj Varma","family":"Lakhamraju","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"container-title":["The AI Audit: Accountability, Integrity, and the Future of Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-8","abstract":"<jats:p>The relationship between HR technology and compliance and audit readiness plays a key role in today\u2019s corporate situation. The chapter looks into how the development of HR technology makes it possible to meet regulations and promote financial transparency. It states that integrated HR systems help follow legal requirements, get ready for audits, and avoid risks. When HR follows the company\u2019s goals, uses new technologies, and decides based on information, it helps the organization maintain quick responses and strong accountability. This chapter points out the main aspects, benefits, issues, and steps to learn how HR technology changes help meet company targets and rules.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6731878","title":["Off-Page Signals Have Model-Specific Effects on Generative AI Search Visibility: Evidence from a Cross-Platform Audit of 2,729 Businesses Across Five Generative AI Systems"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-1799-0133","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Joel","family":"House","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6731878","abstract":"<jats:p>Background. Businesses are increasingly discovered through generative artificial intelligence (AI) search systems such as ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, Google Gemini, and Google AI Overviews. The off-page signals that drive whether a business appears in those answers are not well characterised, and prior work has typically aggregated outcomes across models, masking model-specific behaviour.\n&lt;div&gt;\n Methods. We audited 2,729 businesses across 14 verticals and four metropolitan markets (Los Angeles, New York, Chicago, Sydney). Each business was probed with approximately 95 intent-based prompts across each of five AI systems (ChatGPT GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet, Perplexity sonar-pro, Gemini 2.5-flash, and Google AI Overview via SerpApi), yielding 266,844 paired (business, model, prompt) observations. Each business was enriched with Domain Authority (Moz), organic-traffic and keyword metrics (SpyFu), on-page signals from a direct HTTP crawl (schema, FAQ, indexed pages, blog count, llms.txt), and twelve off-page presence indicators (Reddit, Quora, YouTube, LinkedIn, Wikipedia, Crunchbase, BBB, Yelp, Trustpilot, G2, Capterra, Google Business). We computed Pearson correlations (raw, log-transformed, and Spearman), partial correlations via OLS residualisation, an L2-regularised logistic regression with multicollinearity pruning by variance inflation factor, 95% bootstrap confidence intervals (5,000 resamples), and Benjamini\u2013Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction at q = 0.05.\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n Results. 69.5% of audited businesses (95% CI [67.8%, 71.2%]) were not mentioned by any of the five AI systems; only 1.5% (95% CI [1.06%, 1.98%]) were mentioned by all five. The most novel finding is modelspecific: in per-model strict-isolation analyses (each model treated as a separate binary outcome, with nine controls including Domain Authority and SpyFu metrics), off-page signals including Reddit and Quora mention counts, Wikipedia presence, LinkedIn company pages, and BBB \/ Yelp \/ Trustpilot directory presence had small but robust positive partial correlations with mention probability in Claude (partial r up to +0.219), ChatGPT, and Perplexity, but were not significantly associated with mention probability in Gemini or Google AI Overviews. Of 60 model-by-predictor cells, 34 survived BH-FDR at q &amp;lt; 0.05. In paired within-response analyses, businesses whose URL was cited as a source in a Perplexity response were 6.03\u00d7 more likely to also be mentioned in that response (95% CI [5.62, 6.46]); the corresponding lift for Google AI Overview was 6.64\u00d7 (95% CI [2.48, 11.30]). Domain Authority, the headline predictor in earlier reporting, had a raw Pearson r of 0.337 but its multivariate logisticregression coefficient confidence interval crossed zero (standardised \u03b2 = +0.139, 95% CI [\u22120.031, +0.316]).\n&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n Conclusion. Off-page presence is statistically associated with AI mention probability for three of five major generative AI systems, with effect sizes that are small per individual signal (partial r \u2248 0.10\u20130.22) but consistent across signals. The composite outcome used in earlier reporting averages over models that do and do not respond to off-page signals and consequently understates this relationship. A preregistered controlled trial is planned to test causal magnitude. The dataset and analysis approach are released openly to enable replication. &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;\n &lt;div&gt;&lt;\/div&gt;\n&lt;\/div&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1276-7_4","title":["Time-LLM: Reprogramming Large Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Banglore Vijay Kumar","family":"Vishwas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sri Ram","family":"Macharla","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Time Series Forecasting Using Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1276-7_4"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s44327-026-00268-2","title":["Governance risks of AI reasoning in urban infrastructure through Delphi audit of human and large language model judgment"],"author":[{"given":"Alence","family":"Poudel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Carla","family":"Barrios","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Paola","family":"De La Torre","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Huy","family":"Ton","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Trevor","family":"Surface","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Varenya","family":"Mehta","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Samanata","family":"Silwal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,14]]},"container-title":["Discover Cities"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s44327-026-00268-2"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-64087-2_11","title":["From Large Language Models to Generative AI Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Jakub M.","family":"Tomczak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Deep Generative Modeling"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-64087-2_11"},{"DOI":"10.58178\/257.1065","title":["Compliance &amp; Good Governance FAQ Handbook"],"author":[{"name":"Catalysing Good Governance and Accountability Systems","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.58178\/257.1065","abstract":"<jats:p>From the sessions organised on compliance on L3 certifications on leadereship, FAQ document was generated<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003646501-8","title":["Auditing AI Accountability"],"author":[{"given":"Albert J.","family":"Marcella","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,2]]},"container-title":["Auditing Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003646501-8"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6583999","title":["&lt;span&gt;AI Disclosure Without Accountability: Paper Compliance and the Governance Limits of Transparency in Scientific Research&lt;\/span&gt;"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-9560-3927","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Victor","family":"Frimpong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6583999","abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;span&gt;This paper argues that the institutionalisation of artificial intelligence (AI) disclosure in scientific research has resulted in a type of compliance that emphasises symbolic transparency rather than actual responsibility. Although journals and publishers are progressively mandating that authors disclose their use of AI, these policies remain fragmented, non-standardised, and largely unverifiable. Based on an exploratory review of 80 recent academic articles, the report demonstrates that explicit AI disclosure is limited and, when present, is primarily symbolic or narrative rather than verifiable. This work explains the pattern by introducing the concept of paper compliance and establishing the AI Disclosure Integrity Gap (AIDG), which is defined as the discrepancy between reported AI utilisation and the genuine epistemic impact of AI on research results. The analysis reveals that this disparity is systematically generated by the discordance between transparency-oriented governance frameworks and the iterative, opaque, and irreproducible characteristics of AI-assisted knowledge generation. The study develops testable propositions and introduces the AI Use Traceability Framework (AUTF) as a process-oriented approach for AI governance, emphasising traceability and auditability over transparency. Despite institutional, technical, and incentive-based obstacles that hinder full implementation, traceability provides a means to reduce AIDG and enhance accountability in AI-assisted research. The study advances AI governance and research integrity by treating disclosure as a limited mechanism rather than a complete solution, and by highlighting the risk that current practices create a false sense of transparency.&lt;\/span&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-96-2355-6_3","title":["Large Language Model on Multi-Modal Data"],"author":[{"given":"Avi","family":"Aneja","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anuradha","family":"Dhull","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akansha","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Krishna Kant","family":"Singh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Multimodal Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-96-2355-6_3"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5360444","title":["AI Governance for Risk Modelling in European Banks: A Compliance-First Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Annie","family":"Goswami","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5360444"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003096047","title":["Rising from the Mailroom to the Boardroom"],"author":[{"given":"Bruce R","family":"Turner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2021,7,27]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003096047"},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003652649-12","title":["Integrating Generative AI and Learning Technologies to Redefine Insurance Risk Assessment and Training"],"author":[{"given":"Pooja","family":"Rani","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Simarjeet","family":"Kaur","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,2]]},"container-title":["Large Language Models, AI, and Workforce Transformation"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003652649-12"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6613560","title":["The Board Accountability Gap in AI Cybersecurity: A Proposed Governance Assessment Methodology for AI Cyber Risk at Board Level"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9673-3008","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Terence","family":"Picken","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6613560","abstract":"<jats:p>This paper presents a pre-empirical, model-based framework for AI cybersecurity governance assessment at board level. All quantitative scores, benchmarks, and case outputs are derived from synthetic modelling scenarios and are presented as illustrative estimates pending Phase 2 empirical validation. The methodology is at Phase 1 of a standard three-phase development programme. No proprietary model parameters, coefficients, or derivations are disclosed.\n&lt;div&gt;\n The Picken Diamond\u00ae foundational methodology underpinning this framework is established in Picken, T. (2026), SSRN 6730338.\n&lt;\/div&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781420072488.ch13","title":["Identity Management Audit and Attestation"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2008,4,9]]},"container-title":["Oracle Identity Management"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781420072488.ch13"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/b978-0-443-34073-4.00011-3","title":["AI as muse: How humans can leverage large language model AI for transdisciplinary creative thinking and research"],"author":[{"given":"Ted","family":"Kahn","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anthony","family":"Brandt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence and Creativity"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/b978-0-443-34073-4.00011-3"},{"DOI":"10.1353\/lib.2025.a961196","title":["Toward Generative AI\u2013Driven Metadata Modeling: A Human\u2013Large Language Model Collaborative Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Mayukh","family":"Bagchi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,2]]},"container-title":["Library Trends"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1353\/lib.2025.a961196","abstract":"<jats:p xml:lang=\"en\"> Abstract: For decades, the modeling of metadata has been core to the functioning of any academic library. Metadata\u2019s importance has only increased with the pervasiveness of generative artificial intelligence\u2013driven information activities and services. However, several challenges impact a library metadata model\u2019s reusability, crosswalk, and interoperability with other metadata models. This paper posits that these problems stem from an underlying assumption that there should be only a few core metadata models that would be sufficient for any information service using them, irrespective of the heterogeneity of intradomain or interdomain settings. To that end, this paper advances a contrary view and substantiates its argument in three key steps. First, the paper introduces a novel way of thinking about a library metadata model as an ontology-driven composition of five functionally interlinked representation levels from perception to definition via properties. Second, the paper introduces the representational manifoldness implicit in each of the five levels, which cumulatively contributes to a conceptually entangled library metadata model. Finally, and most importantly, the paper proposes a generative AI\u2013driven, human\u2013large language model collaboration-based metadata modeling approach to disentangle the entanglement inherent in each representation level, which would lead to a conceptually disentangled metadata model. Throughout the paper, the author provides motivating scenarios and examples from libraries handling cancer information.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1093\/9780198945215.003.0047","title":["Bridging the AI Divide"],"author":[{"given":"Fola","family":"Adeleke","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Witwatersrand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,20]]},"container-title":["Oxford Intersections: AI in Society"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/9780198945215.003.0047","abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>While the use of artificial intelligence (AI) technologies in Africa is growing, the disparities in use persist along various dimensions. This article aims to address how enabling governance frameworks can make large language models (LLMs) more inclusive through representation of low-resource languages in training data sets to enable equitable access to information and services. This article assesses emerging governance ecosystems in Africa from the perspective of coloniality and representation in generative AI and the extent to which LLMs can be used to tackle power asymmetries between African data subjects and AI developers to reduce inequalities that the adoption of generative AI may induce in Africa. By assessing the emerging national AI strategies in Africa, this article identifies a gap in AI governance frameworks across Africa specifically in relation to inclusivity in AI development. The countries briefly reviewed are Kenya, South Africa, and Nigeria due to the size and importance of their economies in sub-Saharan Africa and the recent efforts by the governments in these countries to adopt a governance framework to maximize AI technologies in their economies. With the use of case studies and developments across Africa, this article identifies three main data governance areas that will enable equitable generative AI in Africa. These are data generation and collection, regulatory sandboxes, and policy prototypes as well as data sharing. The issues addressed in this article center on data justice and the necessity for visibility, fairness, and representation in the adoption of generative AI across Africa.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9781501520587-002","title":["Chapter 1: The Generative AI Landscape"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,14]]},"container-title":["Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9781501520587-002"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5","title":["Mastering Spring AI"],"author":[{"given":"Banu","family":"Parasuraman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6582319","title":["MAIQ: A Unified Monitoring, Analytics, and Governance Platform for AI and Large Language Model Applications"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-9403-7988","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Gokulanand","family":"M","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-8909-8420","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Gugan","family":"V","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4506-7431","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chandia","family":"S","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6582319","abstract":"<jats:p>Purpose: When an organisation moves LLM-based products into live production, it discovers that experimentation metrics-model accuracy, prompt quality, latency-cover only a fraction of what must be monitored continuously. Cost volatility, safety violations, degraded retrieval relevance, and anomalous interactions all compete for attention simultaneously, yet existing tools were never built for this combination. This paper presents MAIQ (Model AI Intelligence and Quality), a platform that gives AI engineering teams, MLOps practitioners, and governance officers a single, continuously updated view across all of these dimensions at once. Research Limitations\/Implications: The evaluation was conducted on controlled internal workloads. Broader cross-domain benchmarks would strengthen confidence in the performance and accuracy figures reported here, and some governance outcomes are sensitive to how carefully the operator configures the underlying policy rules. Practical Implications: By replacing a patchwork of API logs, billing consoles, and manual scripts with a single platform, MAIQ shortens the time it takes a team to identify the root cause of a production incident. Governance policies can be applied continuously at inference time rather than audited after the fact, and forensic investigations can be launched directly from the dashboard without switching tools.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394271078.ch11","title":["Ethical and Regulatory Compliance Challenges of Generative AI in Human Resources"],"author":[{"given":"Leena","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ankur","family":"Randhelia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ashish","family":"Jain","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Akash Kumar","family":"Choudhary","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,21]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence in Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394271078.ch11"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.175825815.57896005\/v1","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6011-5080","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher Alumnus, International MBA, Bar-Ilan University"},{"name":"Touro College MSIT"},{"name":"Index Terms-Agentic AI, AI Governance, AI Risk Manage-ment, Autonomous Systems, AI Ethics, AI Safety, Regulatory Compliance, Multi-Agent Systems"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,19]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.175825815.57896005\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>The transition from Generative AI to Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) represents a fundamental shift in autonomous systems: from generating content to perceiving, reasoning, acting, and collaborating with minimal human input. This evolution unlocks significant economic potential, with estimates in the trillions of dollars, and introduces practical challenges across safety, security, ethics, and regulatory compliance. Evaluating current governance frameworks-including ISO\/IEC 42001 and the EU AI Act-we find significant gaps in addressing continuous monitoring, human-agent collaboration, and multi-agent coordination. Based on these findings, we propose a multi-layered governance framework that integrates technical safeguards, ethical alignment, and adaptive regulatory compliance. Key pillars include Governance by Design, automated risk detection, and human oversight to mitigate both technical and societal risks. Our analysis highlights that traditional AI governance frameworks, designed for static or narrow AI models, are inadequate for dynamic, multi-agent, goal-oriented systems. We systematically characterize Agentic AI by its core capabilities, multi-step decision-making processes, and architectural components. We identify a comprehensive risk taxonomy encompassing technical failure, adversarial exploitation, emergent unsafe behaviors, and accountability gaps. Evaluating current governance frameworks-including ISO\/IEC 42001 and the EU AI Act-we find significant gaps in addressing continuous monitoring, humanagent collaboration, and multi-agent coordination. Based on these findings, we summarize a multi-layered governance framework that integrates technical safeguards, ethical alignment, and adaptive regulatory compliance. Key pillars include Governance by Design, automated risk detection, and human oversight to mitigate both technical and societal risks. Our findings underscore the need for targeted policy interventions, operational best practices, and focused research on measuring and mitigating the realworld impact of Agentic AI. By bridging technical, ethical, and regulatory dimensions, this work provides actionable guidance for stakeholders aiming to deploy autonomous AI systems safely, responsibly, and effectively.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.173198336.68543189\/v1","title":["The AI Engine of Creation: Exploring the Capabilities of AI Chatbots based on Generative AI, Large Language Models and Large Multimodal Models"],"author":[{"given":"Dishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Birmingham City University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ishita","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King Edward VI Five Ways School"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0659-9646","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nitin","family":"Naik","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Digital Technologies, Aston University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,19]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.173198336.68543189\/v1","abstract":"<jats:p>AI chatbots have been reincarnated with the soul of generative AI, and now they are able to respond with intelligence and consciousness similar to that of humans. These AI chatbots that are based on generative AI are continuously evolving and enhancing their capabilities to act increasingly similar to humans. Generative AI represents all those AI technologies that can generate variety of contents including text, image, audio, video, code, virtual world, dataset, and web data. Different types of generative AI models have been developed to generate such contents, and Large Language Models (LLMs) and Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) are currently the most popular generative AI models used in most modern AI chatbots; where LLMs mostly handle and generate text data, whilst LMMs are able to handle and generate multiple data types or modalities such as text, image, audio, and video. The growing importance of AI chatbots based on generative AI necessitates an exploration of their capabilities in a detailed manner to obtain better understanding about their strengths and utilisations. Exploring the capabilities of AI chatbots that are based on generative AI, this paper will provide a systematic classification and thorough explanation of the capabilities of AI chatbots. It will classify the capabilities of AI chatbots into eight major categories: text generation and analysis, image generation and analysis, audio generation and analysis, video generation and analysis, code generation and analysis, virtual world generation and analysis, data (dataset) generation and analysis, and web data generation and analysis. This systematic classification and thorough exploration of the capabilities of AI chatbots will enable users to analyse these capabilities and the effective utilisation of AI chatbots in real life.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4511540","title":["Large Language Models and Generative AI in Finance: An Analysis of ChatGPT, Bard, and Bing AI"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-7257-9141","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"David","family":"Krause","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4511540","abstract":"<jats:p>The field of finance is constantly evolving, and artificial intelligence (AI) is playing an increasingly important role in this evolution. Large language models (LLMs) and generative AI are two of the most promising AI technologies for the finance industry. This paper examines the application of LLMs and generative AI in the field of finance. It specifically focuses on three AI models: ChatGPT, Bard, and Bing AI. These models have shown significant potential in enhancing financial analysis, automating tasks, and providing valuable insights. By leveraging their language generation capabilities, they streamline financial analysis processes, improve decision-making, and advance natural language processing in finance. The analysis highlights the strengths and limitations of each model and emphasizes the importance of effective prompting and validation of AI-generated outputs. The evaluation of AI models in finance can be conducted using metrics such as ROUGE, BLEU, accuracy, precision, and perplexity. Despite their capabilities, challenges such as contextual understanding, factual accuracy, bias, fairness, and domain adaptation need to be addressed. Future directions for generative AI models in finance include domain adaptation, knowledge integration, explainability, interpretability, and ethical considerations. Generative AI models can be utilized responsibly and effectively in the finance industry; however, this will require careful consideration of the factors discussed in the paper.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/ai6110296","title":["Modeling Dynamic Risk Perception Using Large Language Model (LLM) Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4181-4724","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"He","family":"Wen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"AI Safety Lab, Bailey College of Engineering & Technology, Indiana State University, Terre Haute, IN 47809, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1327-5460","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mojtaba","family":"Parsaee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"College of Architecture, Art and Design, Mississippi State University, Starkville, MS 39762, USA"},{"name":"Data Science Academic Institute, Mississippi State University, Starkville, MS 39762, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4318-4690","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zaman","family":"Sajid","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Mary Kay O\u2019Connor Process Safety Center, Artie McFerrin Department of Chemical Engineering, Texas A&M University, College Station, TX 77843, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,19]]},"container-title":["AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/ai6110296","abstract":"<jats:p>Background: Understanding how accident risk escalates during unfolding industrial events is essential for developing intelligent safety systems. This study proposes a large language model (LLM)-based framework that simulates human-like risk reasoning over sequential accident precursors. Methods: Using 100 investigation reports from the U.S. Chemical Safety Board (CSB), two Generative Pre-trained Transformer (GPT) agents were developed: (1) an Accident Precursor Extractor to identify and classify time-ordered events, and (2) a Subjective Probability Estimator to update perceived accident likelihood as precursors unfold. Results: The subjective accident probability increases near-linearly, with an average escalation of 8.0% \u00b1 0.9% per precursor (p&lt;0.05). A consistent tipping point occurs at the fourth precursor, marking a perceptual shift to high-risk awareness. Across 90 analyzed cases, Agent 1 achieved 0.88 precision and 0.84 recall, while Agent 2 reproduced human-like probabilistic reasoning within \u00b10.08 of expert baselines. The magnitude of escalation differed across precursor types. Organizational factors were perceived as the highest risk (median = 0.56), followed by human error (median = 0.47). Technical and environmental factors demonstrated comparatively smaller effects. Conclusions: These findings confirm that LLM agents can emulate Bayesian-like updating in dynamic risk perception, offering a scalable and explainable foundation for adaptive, sequence-aware safety monitoring in safety-critical systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6003655","title":["AI-Driven Governance, Risk, and Compliance (GRC) Frameworks for Secure Financial Technology Ecosystems"],"author":[{"given":"Chris","family":"Audrey","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6003655"},{"DOI":"10.2196\/90759","title":["Toward Retrieval-Grounded Evaluation for Conversational Large Language Model\u2013Based Risk Assessment"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5857-5783","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yihan","family":"Hu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,12]]},"container-title":["JMIR AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/90759"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-658-44690-1_1-1","title":["Generative AI und Agentenbasierte KI"],"author":[{"given":"Phil","family":"Wennker","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Generative KI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-658-44690-1_1-1"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781394423088.ch01","title":["The Role of Audit in Security Governance, Risk, and Compliance"],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,28]]},"container-title":["Cybersecurity Auditing"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781394423088.ch01"},{"DOI":"10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-7","title":["Audit: Risk Aware Software Security"],"author":[{"name":"Senior Software Engineer, Meta Platforms, USA","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-7539-8799","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Subramanya Shashank gollapudi","family":"Venkata","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,7]]},"container-title":["The AI Audit: Accountability, Integrity, and the Future of Finance"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.48001\/978-81-988770-4-8-7","abstract":"<jats:p>Today, the inability to trust or access software systems can greatly affect a business\u2019s results. Because auditors now manage risks as well as comply with rules, it is important that software security is included in every part of the audit process. Many IT and software audits today use fixed checklists and known security standards instead of considering the particular threats and the results of discovered issues on the business. Risks are considered in software security by directly using risk evaluation systems during planning of audits, tests, and reports.\nIncluding controls testing, risk-weighted sampling, threat modeling, and vulnerability prioritization in their reviews, auditors provide ideas that help improve the GRC position of organizations. This chapter examines ways in which risk issues are addressed by using ISO\/IEC 27001 ,COBIT, and NIST RMF to help audits have a higher quality and wider reach. With the help of examples and analytical tools, the chapter demonstrates that security-focused audits can suggest useful advice and lower the risks facing a company<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5138252","title":["Artificial Intelligence Governance and Regulation; The impact of the EU AI Act, 2024 on Innovation, Accountability, and Global Compliance in a Digital Age"],"author":[{"given":"Uloma","family":"Okoro","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5138252"},{"DOI":"10.65025\/icaic26179c","title":["AI-Driven Compliance Monitoring in the Oil and Gas Industry: Governance Frameworks for Environmental Accountability and Regulatory Oversight"],"author":[{"given":"Jemimah","family":"D. Chuntar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Oil and Gas Lawyer & Director \/ Founding Partner, LIRION Energy; LL.M. in Oil and Gas Law, CEPMLP, University of Dundee, Scotland; Founder, LexPetra AI"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,20]]},"container-title":["ICAIC Conference Proceedings"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.65025\/icaic26179c","abstract":"<jats:p>Artificial Intelligence (AI) is increasingly being deployed across the oil and gas industry to support environmental monitoring, predictive risk analysis, spill detection, and automated regulatory reporting. While these technologies present opportunities to significantly improve operational transparency and regulatory compliance, they also introduce new governance challenges, particularly in jurisdictions where environmental accountability and data transparency remain ongoing concerns. Building on earlier research examining the ethical implications of AI in extractive industries, this paper explores the governance structures required to ensure responsible AI deployment within oil and gas regulatory ecosystems. Using Nigeria's petroleum regulatory framework as a case study, the study examines how AI-enabled systems\u2014including natural language processing models for regulatory document analysis and machine learning tools for environmental incident monitoring\u2014can strengthen compliance oversight and improve the detection of environmental violations such as oil spills and reporting inconsistencies. However, the integration of AI into regulatory processes also raises concerns regarding algorithmic bias, data governance integrity, explainability of automated decision systems, and institutional dependence on opaque technological infrastructures. This paper proposes a governance-centered framework for AI adoption in extractive industries that integrates legal oversight, transparent algorithmic auditing, and responsible data management practices. By situating AI deployment within broader regulatory and governance structures, this research highlights the critical role of law and policy in shaping accountable and trustworthy AI systems capable of enhancing environmental stewardship, regulatory compliance, and public trust in resource-dependent economies.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5200768","title":["Large Language Scholarship: Generative AI in the Legal Academy"],"author":[{"given":"Alan Z.","family":"Rozenshtein","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kevin","family":"Frazier","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5200768"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5155269","title":["Prospect of Internet of Things using Generative AI and Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Ajit","family":"Singh","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5155269"},{"DOI":"10.1002\/9781119380498.ch7","title":["Cyber Risk Role in Governance Model and Compliance Framework"],"author":[{"given":"Alexander","family":"Abramov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2018,4,26]]},"container-title":["Governance, Compliance, and Supervision in the Capital Markets"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/9781119380498.ch7"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5511060","title":["Framework for Government Policy on Agentic and Generative AI: Governance, Regulation, and Risk Management"],"author":[{"given":"Satyadhar","family":"Joshi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5511060"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-96-0029-8_9","title":["Large-Scale Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Takeshi","family":"Okadome","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Essentials of Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-96-0029-8_9"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781003096047-11","title":["Holding Court at the Audit Committee Table"],"author":[{"given":"Bruce R","family":"Turner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2021,7,27]]},"container-title":["Rising from the Mailroom to the Boardroom"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781003096047-11"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5911464","title":["Governing AI Agents:&amp;nbsp;Risk, Compliance, and Accountability in Law and Finance"],"author":[{"given":"Jillian","family":"Bommarito","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Daniel Martin","family":"Katz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2236-1187","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Michael James","family":"Bommarito","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5911464","abstract":"<jats:p>Agentic AI systems\u2014capable of autonomous goal pursuit, environmental perception, and iterative action\u2014are rapidly entering legal and financial services, yet existing governance frameworks were not designed for software that acts rather than merely responds. Unlike passive AI tools, agentic systems can stray from their intended objectives and take consequential actions without human approval, creating risks that traditional compliance models often fail to address. This chapter proposes a governance framework that can be used to scale oversight requirements to match each system's risk profile, based on factors like degree of autonomy, how long it operates, whose interests it serves, and how its objectives are set and modified. The framework situates agentic AI within a five-layer regulatory stack encompassing foundational law, professional ethics, sector-specific regulation, emerging AI-specific rules (including the EU AI Act), and voluntary assurance standards such as NIST and SOC 2.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The chapter argues that in law and finance\u2014industries built on trust and non-delegable professional duties\u2014human-in-the-loop and human-in-command architectures are not merely best practices but critical designs for satisfying fiduciary and regulatory obligations. To operationalize accountability without fragmenting ownership, the chapter evaluates three organizational models\u2014centralized, federated, and embedded\u2014and demonstrates how responsibility-assignment tools such as RACI matrices can allocate oversight duties with precision. It concludes by outlining a maturity-based adoption path that enables legal and financial institutions to adopt agentic AI while managing liability exposure and reputational risk.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3389\/fpsyt.2025.1544951","title":["Effectiveness of generative AI-large language models\u2019 recognition of veteran suicide risk: a comparison with human mental health providers using a risk stratification model"],"author":[{"given":"Sean A.","family":"Lauderdale","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Randee","family":"Schmitt","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Breanna","family":"Wuckovich","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Natashaa","family":"Dalal","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hela","family":"Desai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shealyn","family":"Tomlinson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,3]]},"container-title":["Frontiers in Psychiatry"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3389\/fpsyt.2025.1544951","abstract":"<jats:sec><jats:title>Background<\/jats:title><jats:p>With over 6,300 United States military veterans dying by suicide annually, the Veterans Health Administration (VHA) is exploring innovative strategies, including artificial intelligence (AI), for suicide risk assessment. Machine learning has been predominantly utilized, but the application of generative AI-large language models (GAI-LLMs) remains unexplored.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec><jats:sec><jats:title>Objective<\/jats:title><jats:p>This study evaluates the effectiveness of GAI-LLMs, specifically ChatGPT-3.5, ChatGPT-4o, and Google Gemini, in using the VHA\u2019s Risk Stratification Table for identifying suicide risks and making treatment recommendations in response to standardized veteran vignettes.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec><jats:sec><jats:title>Methods<\/jats:title><jats:p>We compared the GAI-LLMs\u2019 assessments and recommendations for both acute and chronic suicide risks to evaluations by mental health care providers (MHCPs). Four vignettes, representing varying levels of suicide risk, were used.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec><jats:sec><jats:title>Results<\/jats:title><jats:p>GAI-LLMs\u2019 assessments showed discrepancies with MHCPs, particularly rating the most acute case as less acute and the least acute case as more acute. For chronic risk, GAI-LLMs\u2019 evaluations were generally in line with MHCPs, except for one vignette rated with higher chronic risk by the GAI-LLM. Variation across GAI-LLMs was also observed. Notably, ChatGPT-3.5 showed lower acute risk ratings compared to ChatGPT-4o and Google Gemini, while ChatGPT-4o identified higher chronic risk ratings and recommended hospitalization for all veterans. Treatment planning by GAI-LLMs was predicted by chronic but not acute risk ratings.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec><jats:sec><jats:title>Conclusion<\/jats:title><jats:p>While GAI-LLMs offers potential suicide risk assessment comparable to MHCPs, significant variation exists across different GAI-LLMs in both risk evaluation and treatment recommendations. Continued MHCP oversight is essential to ensure accuracy and appropriate care.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec><jats:sec><jats:title>Implications<\/jats:title><jats:p>These findings highlight the need for further research into optimizing GAI-LLMs for consistent and reliable use in clinical settings, ensuring they complement rather than replace human expertise.<\/jats:p><\/jats:sec>"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-2733-4_14","title":["Governance, Compliance, and Security"],"author":[{"given":"Ajay","family":"Rawat","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vardan","family":"Pathak","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Mastering Large Language Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-2733-4_14"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0403-8_6","title":["Generative Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Shivam R","family":"Solanki","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Drupad K","family":"Khublani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Generative Artificial Intelligence"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0403-8_6"},{"DOI":"10.1177\/02655322261458364","title":["Assessing Interactional Competence Through Generative AI: Comparing Large Language Models as AI Interlocutors in the Paired Oral Discussion Test"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5360-2772","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Inyoung","family":"Na","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Iowa State University, Ames, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,8]]},"container-title":["Language Testing"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/02655322261458364","abstract":"<jats:p>Interactional competence (IC) is essential for oral communication assessment, yet human partner variability can introduce construct-irrelevant variance in paired speaking tests. As an alternative to a test with a human interlocutor, this study describes the development of a large language model (LLM)-driven Spoken Dialogue System and compares GPT-4o to Claude 3.5 Sonnet to inform model selection for IC assessment. Twelve international students completed paired discussion tasks with both LLMs in counterbalanced order. System performance was evaluated through breakdown activation consistency, stance maintenance, and persona adherence. Test-taker performances were analyzed using interactional discourse analysis to identify IC features across three dimensions: topic management, interactional management, and interactive listening. Semi-structured interviews explored test takers\u2019 perceptions of the AI partners. Results showed Claude outperformed GPT-4o in eliciting IC features, successfully activating communication breakdown strategies and maintaining oppositional stance, thereby creating more opportunities for test takers to demonstrate key IC abilities. Test takers perceived Claude as more authentic and natural, while GPT was perceived as more artificial. These findings demonstrate that different LLMs create distinct interactional conditions affecting both IC elicitation and test-taker perceptions. The findings highlight the need for construct-driven evaluation criteria when selecting LLMs for language-assessment contexts.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4236\/jsea.2026.196010","title":["AI Selection in Organizations: A Decision Framework for Large Language Model and Generative AI Deployments"],"author":[{"given":"Andrew","family":"Ganje","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Journal of Software Engineering and Applications"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4236\/jsea.2026.196010"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_5","title":["Conversational AI with Spring AI"],"author":[{"given":"Banu","family":"Parasuraman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Mastering Spring AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-1001-5_5"},{"DOI":"10.55248\/gengpi.6.0225.0718","title":["AI-Enhanced Cybersecurity Audits in IT Governance: Strengthening Risk Management,  Compliance, and Threat Intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Rasheed","family":"Agboluaje","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,2]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Research Publication and Reviews"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.55248\/gengpi.6.0225.0718"},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9788743808145-5","title":["LLM \u2013 Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Sudha","family":"Jamthe","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,12]]},"container-title":["Generative AI"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9788743808145-5"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}